<p class="lt-chem-371677">Another deviation occurs when a subshell becomes more than half filled. For example, oxygen&rsquo;s IE<sub>1</sub> is slightly lower than that for nitrogen. The orbital diagram of oxygen shows that the last electron added (red) is forced to pair with another electron, because the 2<em>p</em> subshell is more than half full.</p>

<p class="mt-align-center lt-chem-371677"><img alt="Orbital diagram of oxygen showing two arrows, one up and one down, representing electrons, in the 1s tier, two arrows in the 2s tier and 2 arrows in the 2p tier, each occupying a different one of the three sections of the 2p tier and 2 arrows, both in the third section of the 2p tier." class="internal default" style="width: 437px; height: 208px;" width="437px" height="208px" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/409929/Oxygen_EConfig_Orbital_Diagram_Red_Electron-1024x486.png?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=437&amp;height=208" /></p>

<p class="lt-chem-371677">Loss of the electron (colored red in the orbital diagram) yields a greater reduction of electron&ndash;electron repulsion because there are no longer two electrons in the same orbital. This makes it easier to lose one electron from O and the ionization energy is smaller than expected. The same explanation applies to the dip for sulfur after phosphorus in Figure 1.</p>

<p class="lt-chem-371677">Looking at the successive IEs in the <a rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank" title="6.5: Ionization Energies" href="/Under_Construction/Wisconsin_Chemistry_(Moore_Zhou_and_Garand)/06:_Appendix/6.05:_Ionization_Energies">appendix</a>, we see that for any element, IE<sub>1</sub> &lt; IE<sub>2</sub> &lt; IE<sub>3</sub>, and so forth, and this is due to the greater <em>Z</em><sub>eff</sub> in successively more positive ions. However, sometimes the increase in successive IE is larger than expected. For example, consider the data in Table 1:</p>

